// heartsim patient case: anterior ST-elevation infarction with ventricular fibrillation. Original text by
// the heartsim authors, MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/case.rs.
Case(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "case_anterior_stemi_arrest",
    title: "Crushing pain, then a collapse",
    summary: "A man in his sixties arrives with severe chest pain. Read the first ECG, meet the rhythm that can follow, and follow the heart to the next day.",
    patient: "63-year-old man, a lorry-yard manager, one hour of crushing central chest pain with sweating, ex-smoker, no known heart disease.",
    references: [
        "Savas G et al. R-on-T-triggered ventricular fibrillation during acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Eur Heart J Case Rep 2026;10(5). doi:10.1093/ehjcr/ytag342 (open access, CC BY 4.0; used for the clinical pattern only).",
        "Zeppenfeld K et al. 2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death. Eur Heart J 2022;43:3997-4126. doi:10.1093/eurheartj/ehac262 (contents and figure titles read only).",
    ],
    stages: [
        Stage(
            title: "The first ECG",
            markdown: r#"
**Mr Haddad** is grey, sweating and clutching his chest. His pulse is 78 and regular and his blood
pressure is 134/86 mmHg. The 12-lead ECG shows sinus rhythm with marked **ST-segment elevation across
the chest leads and in leads I and aVL**, and ST depression in III and aVF.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("stemi_anterior")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which territory does this pattern fit?",
                    choices: [
                        "Inferior wall",
                        "Anterior wall, septum and apex",
                        "Posterior wall only",
                        "No territory: it is diffuse",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "ST elevation in V1 to V6 with I and aVL looks at the front of the left ventricle, the septum and the apex. Inferior elevation would be in II, III and aVF.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which artery is the most likely culprit?",
                    choices: [
                        "The right coronary artery",
                        "The left circumflex artery",
                        "The left anterior descending artery, probably proximal",
                        "The posterior descending artery",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "The left anterior descending artery supplies the anterior wall and septum. Elevation that reaches I and aVL suggests the blockage lies before the first diagonal branch, which means a large area at risk.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "What is the ST depression in III and aVF?",
                    choices: [
                        "A second infarct in the inferior wall",
                        "Digoxin effect",
                        "A sign of pericarditis",
                        "A reciprocal change, the mirror image of the anterior injury",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "Leads on the opposite side of the heart see the injury current with the opposite sign. Reciprocal depression supports a true infarct and, here, a proximal occlusion. Pericarditis does not give reciprocal depression, apart from aVR.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "The collapse",
            markdown: r#"
While he waits for the catheter laboratory, the monitor shows ventricular ectopic beats. One of them
falls on the **T wave** of the preceding beat. In published cases an ectopic beat at that moment has
started ventricular fibrillation, and Mr Haddad slumps, unresponsive.

The simulator cannot turn this infarct rhythm into fibrillation by itself, so it now **loads the arrest
rhythm** instead: a few seconds of sinus rhythm, and then fibrillation. The ST elevation of the
infarct is not drawn on this strip; the infarct is still there in the story.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("vf_coarse")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(SelectLead(II)),
                Ui(WaitForEvent(ElapsedMs(5000.0))),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What does the strip show now?",
                    choices: [
                        "Ventricular fibrillation: chaotic deflections with no QRS complexes",
                        "A baseline wander artefact from a loose lead",
                        "Torsades de pointes",
                        "Complete heart block",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "There are no recognisable P waves, QRS complexes or T waves, only irregular deflections. A patient in VF is unconscious and has no pulse; a loose lead would leave the patient awake. Check the patient first, then the leads.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "Ventricular arrhythmias are common in the first hours of an acute infarct. What does that mean for the care of this patient?",
                    choices: [
                        "Reperfusion should wait until the rhythm is stable",
                        "He should be monitored continuously with a defibrillator at hand, and reperfusion is not delayed",
                        "Monitoring can stop once the ECG is recorded",
                        "Adenosine should be given to prevent them",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "The early arrhythmias are a reason for monitoring and for a defibrillator within reach. They do not change the need to open the artery as soon as possible. Neither adenosine nor stopping monitoring prevents them.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "The shock",
            markdown: r#"
He is not breathing and has no pulse. Chest compressions are started (the simulator has no
compressions), and the defibrillator is charged. The simulator now delivers an **unsynchronised shock
of 200 J** to the fibrillating heart.

A simulated shock can fail: its outcome is drawn from a seeded random generator. In our test run the
first shock restored a regular rhythm. Look at the strip to see what happened in yours; the next page
starts from the rhythm after the shock whatever you saw.
"#,
            actions: [
                Sim(Shock(joules: 200.0, synchronised: false)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Which kind of shock is right for ventricular fibrillation?",
                    choices: [
                        "A synchronised shock at a low energy",
                        "A synchronised shock at maximum energy",
                        "An unsynchronised shock, given as soon as the defibrillator is ready",
                        "No shock until a pulse check",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "VF has no R wave to synchronise to, so the shock is unsynchronised and is given as early as possible.",
                ),
                Question(
                    prompt: "What would the simulator's defibrillator do if it were left in synchronised mode during VF?",
                    choices: [
                        "Fire at once, at the lowest energy",
                        "Fire on the next chaotic deflection",
                        "Charge but never fire, because it waits for an R wave that does not exist",
                        "Switch to unsynchronised mode by itself",
                    ],
                    answer: 2,
                    explanation: "A defibrillator in synchronised mode is designed to fire only on an R wave, and the simulator's does the same.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "Back to the infarct",
            markdown: r#"
Whatever the strip showed, the simulator now loads the **infarct rhythm again**: sinus at about 78 per
minute and the same ST elevation as in the first ECG. The artery has not opened; only the arrhythmia
has been dealt with. Mr Haddad is breathing and his pulse is back.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("stemi_anterior")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "Sinus rhythm is back after the shock. What does the ST elevation that is still present tell you?",
                    choices: [
                        "The shock caused it",
                        "It means nothing now that the rhythm is normal",
                        "That the infarct is old",
                        "That the artery is still blocked and he still needs reperfusion",
                    ],
                    answer: 3,
                    explanation: "The shock treats the rhythm, not the cause. The occluded artery is still injuring the muscle and the ST segments stay raised until the artery is opened.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "After reperfusion",
            markdown: r#"
The artery is opened in the catheter laboratory. In the simulator the **stenosis of the left anterior
descending artery is removed**. After a short time the ST segments settle towards the baseline.
"#,
            actions: [
                Sim(Stenosis(branch: "LAD", severity: 0.0)),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What does the settling of the ST segments suggest?",
                    choices: [
                        "Successful reperfusion of the infarct-related artery",
                        "That no muscle was lost",
                        "A new infarct in another territory",
                        "That the ECG leads were reversed",
                    ],
                    answer: 0,
                    explanation: "ST resolution is a marker of reperfusion. It does not prove that no muscle was lost, and the heart is still at risk of arrhythmia for some time.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
        Stage(
            title: "The next day",
            markdown: r#"
The following morning his rhythm is regular and he is pain-free. The ST elevation has gone, but the
ECG has changed again: the simulator now shows the **evolved infarct** pattern, with **Q or QS
complexes and inverted T waves over the front of the heart** (V1 to V4, and aVL).
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("mi_anterior_evolved")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(TwelveLead)),
            ],
            questions: [
                Question(
                    prompt: "What do the new Q waves in V1 to V4 represent?",
                    choices: [
                        "Pericarditis",
                        "A region of muscle that no longer produces electrical forces, so the leads over it see the opposite wall",
                        "Reperfusion injury of the right ventricle",
                        "Lead misplacement",
                    ],
                    answer: 1,
                    explanation: "Dead or stunned tissue gives no forces, so a lead over it looks through the hole at the activation of the far wall and records a negative deflection. Q waves usually persist, while the ST segment and T wave change over hours and days.",
                ),
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
